<h1>Result Fields</h1>
<p>A status says whether a case was right. A <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/">result
	field</a> says what the case measured. Fields are declared on a result class the way configuration fields are
	declared on a config class, and every value a case assigns to one is carried into the log, the execution tree and
	the Details dialog. This page uses the <strong>Result Fields</strong> test of the
	<strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group, which follows
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/checkpoints/">Checkpoints</a>.</p>
<p>Select the <strong>Result Fields</strong> test and set its <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/result-fields/result-fields-config.png", "Result Fields run panel with the Maze Executable Folder set, reporting Total Cases 12 across the width, height and seed dimensions", "large") }}
<p>The matrix combines two widths, two heights and three seeds into 12 valid <code>solve</code> cases. Nothing in
	this test is expected to fail. What it adds is three values per case: the maze area, the length of the path the
	application found, and the ratio between them.</p>

<h2 id="declaring-result-fields">Declaring result fields</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_statuses_and_results/test_result_fields/") }}
<p>The test declares a result class next to its config class. Each field comes from the
	<code>OrcraftResultField</code> namespace and carries a default and a label:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('class TestResultFieldsResult(OrcraftTestResult):
	maze_area = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=None, label="Maze Area")
	path_length = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=None, label="Path Length")
	area_path_ratio = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=None, label="Area / Path Ratio")') }}
<p>The label is what the log and the controller show. A field without a label stays internal, and the other
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/#result-fields">field types and options</a> are listed in
	the framework reference.</p>

<h3 id="assigning-values">Assigning values</h3>
<p>The case body starts by giving the case an instance of that result class, then hands the case to the suite's
	Maze helper:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> TestResultFieldsResult:
	case.result = TestResultFieldsResult()

	maze_app = MazeApp(
		self.config.maze_executable_folder,
		"solve",
		width=case.params["width"],
		height=case.params["height"],
		seed=case.params["seed"],
	)

	maze_app.run_and_validate(case)

	return case.result') }}
<p>The helper runs the application, reads the step count from its output and assigns the fields. It also raises a
	warning when the path is long relative to the maze:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def run_and_validate(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> None:
	result = self._execute()
	maze_area = (self.width or 0) * (self.height or 0)
	case.result.maze_area = maze_area
	if result.solved:
		path_length = result.steps
		area_path_ratio = round(maze_area / path_length, 2)
		case.result.path_length = path_length
		case.result.area_path_ratio = area_path_ratio
		if area_path_ratio < 1.8:
			case.result.warning_description = (
				f"Area/path ratio is {area_path_ratio}, which is less than 1.8. "
				"The solution path is long relative to the maze area."
			)') }}
<p>A result field is assigned like any attribute. The warning uses the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/orcraft-statuses/#pass-with-warning"><code>warning_description</code></a>
	seen earlier, so the ratio both appears as a value and, when it is low, flags the case.</p>

<h2 id="values-in-the-log">Values in the log</h2>
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, then <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and
	confirm. When the run finishes, search the log for <code>Area / Path Ratio</code> and click
	<strong>Next</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/result-fields/case-result-in-log.png", "Log of the second Result Fields case showing the solved maze, the Path found line and the case result table with Maze Area 75, Path Length 47 and Area / Path Ratio 1.6 beneath the status and warning description", "large") }}
<p>Every case ends with a result table, and the three declared fields appear in it under their labels, after the
	case's dimensions, status and warning. Here a 5 by 15 maze solved in 47 steps gives a ratio of 1.6, low enough
	for the warning line above the fields.</p>
<p>Clear the search field to return to the end of the log:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/result-fields/running-result-fields-test.png", "Completed Result Fields run with 12 done and 12 passed, the log showing the test result with 6 cases with warnings and a Warnings table listing those cases", "large") }}
<p>The test passes with all 12 cases and reports 6 cases with warnings. The <strong>Warnings</strong> table lists
	them with their descriptions, so the low ratios can be found without opening each case.</p>

<h2 id="values-in-the-details-dialog">Values in the Details dialog</h2>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the run. The declared fields become columns of the results tree, after the
	columns every result carries:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/result-fields/result-fields-details.png", "Result Fields Details view widened to show the Maze Area, Path Length and Area / Path Ratio columns at the right end of the results tree, with a value for every case and case cycle", "large") }}
<p>Each case and case cycle row carries its own <strong>Maze Area</strong>, <strong>Path Length</strong> and
	<strong>Area / Path Ratio</strong>. The larger mazes at the bottom of the tree have the higher ratios and no
	warning, and the small ones at the top have the low ratios the warnings pointed at. The columns are part of the
	dialog's <strong>Export to XLSX</strong> as well, which is how measured values leave the controller for further
	analysis.</p>

<p>This completes the <strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group. The next group,
	<strong>Structure and Lifecycle</strong>, starts with
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/structure-and-lifecycle/">Structure and Lifecycle</a>: composing a test
	from steps and flows, and the setup and teardown hooks that run around a test, a cycle and a case.</p>
